1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,962
Let me start with a simple question.

2
00:00:02,002 --> 00:00:05,542
How much time do you think a Procurement
Manager spends every week just

3
00:00:05,582 --> 00:00:09,500
moving the data around, opening an
ERP, copying numbers into Excel,

4
00:00:09,604 --> 00:00:12,961
checking stock levels, following
up with suppliers and preparing the

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:14,672
same reports again and again?

6
00:00:14,712 --> 00:00:18,541
Let's say it's around 12 hours
a week, that's 576 hours a year.

7
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:21,969
Think about that for a moment, that's
almost three and a half months of

8
00:00:22,009 --> 00:00:24,955
working time spent on tasks
that are mostly repetitive.

9
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,540
Now, throughout this course, we are
going to follow someone called Sarah

10
00:00:28,580 --> 00:00:31,947
Mitchell. Sarah manages procurement
and inventory at a company called

11
00:00:31,987 --> 00:00:32,951
BrightLeaf Supplies.

12
00:00:32,991 --> 00:00:36,691
It's a distributor of industrial
and packaging supplies based in

13
00:00:36,731 --> 00:00:40,657
Columbus, Ohio. Sarah is fictional,
but the situation probably is not

14
00:00:40,697 --> 00:00:43,981
unfamiliar to you. In fact, some
of you watching this might be doing

15
00:00:44,021 --> 00:00:45,639
exactly the same things every week.

16
00:00:45,679 --> 00:00:47,446
And that's what we are going to change.

17
00:00:47,486 --> 00:00:51,506
Over the next 10 videos, we are
going to build an AI agent that can

18
00:00:51,546 --> 00:00:55,176
connect to an ERP, check the
inventory, look at external information

19
00:00:55,216 --> 00:00:59,019
like weather and exchange rates,
record its decision and even prepare

20
00:00:59,059 --> 00:01:01,938
purchase requests. We are going
to call this agent Stocky.

21
00:01:02,010 --> 00:01:05,230
And the interesting part, we are
going to build it without writing a

22
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:06,324
single line of code.

23
00:01:06,364 --> 00:01:09,484
Before we get into the actual building,
I want you to understand three

24
00:01:09,524 --> 00:01:13,030
things. First, what's the difference
between a chatbot, a normal

25
00:01:13,070 --> 00:01:14,610
automation, and an AI agent?

26
00:01:14,671 --> 00:01:17,332
Second, what actually goes
into making an AI agent?

27
00:01:17,372 --> 00:01:20,472
And finally, how are we going to
build the whole thing throughout this

28
00:01:20,512 --> 00:01:23,723
course? And don't worry if you are
not from a technical background,

29
00:01:23,763 --> 00:01:26,956
you don't need to know the programming
to follow along, you already

30
00:01:26,996 --> 00:01:29,518
understand your business
processes, that's important part.

31
00:01:29,558 --> 00:01:33,132
I will introduce the technical
concepts as we go, using examples from

32
00:01:33,172 --> 00:01:34,702
everyday supply chain operations.

33
00:01:34,804 --> 00:01:37,824
So, rather than spending too much
time talking about what we are going

34
00:01:37,864 --> 00:01:41,666
to build, let me show you the finished
version first, let's meet Stocky.

35
00:01:42,747 --> 00:01:44,767
It's Monday, seven in the morning.

36
00:01:44,807 --> 00:01:48,067
Sarah hasn't even had her coffee
yet, and there's already an email

37
00:01:48,107 --> 00:01:50,547
waiting for her. It's from Stocky.

38
00:01:50,587 --> 00:01:53,603
Let's open it. This is the
whole course in one email.

39
00:01:54,079 --> 00:01:58,279
Stocky looked inside BrightLeaf's
ERP and checked all ten products.

40
00:01:58,319 --> 00:02:01,719
It found four that have dropped
below their reorder point.

41
00:02:01,759 --> 00:02:05,779
For each one, it tells Sarah how
many are on hand, what the reorder

42
00:02:05,819 --> 00:02:10,299
point is, who the supplier is, how
much to order and roughly what it

43
00:02:10,339 --> 00:02:14,041
costs. But look at number
three, the pallet jack wheels.

44
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:16,261
Stocky didn't just say "you're low".

45
00:02:16,301 --> 00:02:19,721
It noticed that the supplier is in
Shenzhen, it checked the weather

46
00:02:19,761 --> 00:02:23,781
there and saw heavy storms hit Shenzhen
over the weekend, it checked the

47
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:27,221
calendar and saw Thanksgiving next
week, when freight bookings slow

48
00:02:27,261 --> 00:02:31,361
right down, and it knows the lead
time is already twenty-one days.

49
00:02:31,401 --> 00:02:34,041
So it says: order this one today.

50
00:02:34,081 --> 00:02:35,241
That's not a report.

51
00:02:35,281 --> 00:02:37,271
That's a colleague giving you advice.

52
00:02:37,539 --> 00:02:39,659
And at the bottom, two buttons.

53
00:02:39,699 --> 00:02:44,359
Approve. Decline. Stocky has prepared
the purchase requests, but it will

54
00:02:44,399 --> 00:02:47,639
not send anything until Sarah says yes.

55
00:02:47,679 --> 00:02:48,879
Hold on to that idea.

56
00:02:48,919 --> 00:02:51,209
It's important, and we'll come back to it.

57
00:02:51,349 --> 00:02:52,929
So how did that happen?

58
00:02:52,969 --> 00:02:54,889
This is what's behind the curtain.

59
00:02:54,929 --> 00:02:59,829
It's a tool called n8n, and
every box you see is one step.

60
00:02:59,869 --> 00:03:01,309
There's no code here.

61
00:03:01,349 --> 00:03:05,159
You build this by dragging boxes
onto the screen and filling in forms.

62
00:03:05,559 --> 00:03:10,259
Left to right: a timer that says
"every Monday at six fifty-five AM,

63
00:03:10,299 --> 00:03:15,179
start". Then a box that pulls stock
levels from the ERP, which for

64
00:03:15,219 --> 00:03:17,939
BrightLeaf is a system called Odoo.

65
00:03:17,979 --> 00:03:22,179
The big box in the middle is
the agent, the part that thinks.

66
00:03:22,219 --> 00:03:26,619
These three check the weather,
exchange rates and public holidays.

67
00:03:26,659 --> 00:03:29,519
This one writes every
decision into a Google Sheet.

68
00:03:29,559 --> 00:03:32,897
And the last one emails Sarah
and waits for her click.

69
00:03:33,217 --> 00:03:34,757
Here's that record.

70
00:03:34,797 --> 00:03:38,877
Every Monday, every product Stocky
flagged, what it suggested and who

71
00:03:38,917 --> 00:03:40,877
approved it. When Mr.

72
00:03:40,917 --> 00:03:45,337
Harrison, BrightLeaf's operations
director, asks "why did we order those

73
00:03:45,377 --> 00:03:48,097
wheels early?", Sarah
doesn't have to remember.

74
00:03:48,137 --> 00:03:50,327
It's written down. Now watch.

75
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:51,767
Sarah clicks Approve.

76
00:03:51,807 --> 00:03:56,107
And over in Odoo, under Purchase,
Requests for Quotation, there they

77
00:03:56,147 --> 00:04:00,407
are. Four draft requests, one for
each supplier, already filled in with

78
00:04:00,447 --> 00:04:02,277
the right products and quantities.

79
00:04:02,645 --> 00:04:06,845
What used to take Sarah most of her
Monday morning took ninety seconds.

80
00:04:06,885 --> 00:04:10,765
And here's the part I want you to
hear: you are going to build this.

81
00:04:10,805 --> 00:04:12,585
Not watch me build it.

82
00:04:12,625 --> 00:04:14,425
One piece per video.

83
00:04:14,465 --> 00:04:17,955
By video nine, your Stocky
sends this exact email.

84
00:04:18,750 --> 00:04:21,055
Now, let's go back to Sarah for a moment.

85
00:04:21,095 --> 00:04:23,096
We have just seen what Stocky can do.

86
00:04:23,136 --> 00:04:26,814
But what did Sarah's Monday actually
look like before she had it?

87
00:04:26,854 --> 00:04:29,604
She comes into work at
around 8.30 in the morning.

88
00:04:29,738 --> 00:04:34,038
First, she opens Odoo, checks the
inventory report, and exports the data

89
00:04:34,078 --> 00:04:37,679
into Excel. Then she opens the
last week spreadsheet, updates the

90
00:04:37,719 --> 00:04:41,704
numbers, check the formulas, and
fixes whatever happens to the broken

91
00:04:41,794 --> 00:04:45,830
that week. After that, she goes to
the product, identifies what's below

92
00:04:45,870 --> 00:04:48,901
the reorder point, and starts
following up with the suppliers about

93
00:04:48,941 --> 00:04:50,091
their delivery timelines.

94
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:54,410
Remember, she's doing all of this
while also preparing the weekly report

95
00:04:54,450 --> 00:04:56,710
for her operations director, Mr.

96
00:04:56,750 --> 00:04:59,961
Harrison. By around 11, she's
finally ready to send the report.

97
00:05:00,001 --> 00:05:04,115
But then, Tom, the warehouse lead,
walks in and tell her something

98
00:05:04,305 --> 00:05:07,299
unexpected. Sarah, we're
out of stretch wrap.

99
00:05:07,876 --> 00:05:09,677
The Dubai order can't ship.

100
00:05:11,283 --> 00:05:13,159
Now, here's the interesting part.

101
00:05:13,199 --> 00:05:15,966
The stretch wrap was actually
in her inventory report.

102
00:05:16,006 --> 00:05:19,631
The stock was low, it was highlighted
in red, but it was somewhere

103
00:05:19,671 --> 00:05:24,282
around row 41 in a 60 rows per
sheet, and she simply missed it while

104
00:05:24,322 --> 00:05:27,226
working through everything else,
and that's something worth thinking

105
00:05:27,266 --> 00:05:29,336
about. Sarah is not bad at her job.

106
00:05:29,376 --> 00:05:31,236
She's actually doing
a lot of things right.

107
00:05:31,276 --> 00:05:35,286
The problem is that she's spending
so much time collecting information,

108
00:05:35,556 --> 00:05:39,128
updating spreadsheets and checking
numbers that she has less time to

109
00:05:39,168 --> 00:05:40,560
actually analyze what's happening.

110
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:42,634
And that's where an AI agent can help.

111
00:05:42,674 --> 00:05:46,663
We are not trying to replace Sarah
or take away her decision making.

112
00:05:46,703 --> 00:05:50,743
We are trying to remove some of the
repetitive work so she can focus on

113
00:05:50,783 --> 00:05:53,073
the things that actually
need her attention.

114
00:05:53,471 --> 00:05:56,791
That's the idea behind Stocky and
that's what we will be building

115
00:05:56,831 --> 00:05:57,821
throughout this course.

116
00:05:58,633 --> 00:06:02,753
Okay. Three words get thrown around
like they mean the same thing:

117
00:06:02,793 --> 00:06:05,533
chatbot, automation and agent.

118
00:06:05,573 --> 00:06:08,943
They don't. Let me show you the
difference with a real test.

119
00:06:09,391 --> 00:06:13,411
First, a chatbot. This is Claude,
one of the AI assistants we'll use in

120
00:06:13,451 --> 00:06:16,871
this course. ChatGPT works the same way.

121
00:06:16,911 --> 00:06:21,211
I'll ask it: how many steel hex bolts
do we have in stock at BrightLeaf

122
00:06:21,251 --> 00:06:22,961
right now, and should I reorder?

123
00:06:23,413 --> 00:06:24,653
And look at the answer.

124
00:06:24,693 --> 00:06:28,673
It's polite, it's smart, and it
says: I don't have access to your

125
00:06:28,713 --> 00:06:32,323
inventory system, please share
your stock level and reorder point.

126
00:06:32,595 --> 00:06:36,495
That's a chatbot. A brilliant brain,
sitting in a room with no windows

127
00:06:36,535 --> 00:06:40,455
and no doors. It can only think
about what you type into it.

128
00:06:40,495 --> 00:06:41,875
It can't open Odoo.

129
00:06:41,915 --> 00:06:43,395
It can't send an email.

130
00:06:43,435 --> 00:06:45,065
It talks. That's all.

131
00:06:45,585 --> 00:06:46,945
Second, automation.

132
00:06:46,985 --> 00:06:50,285
A workflow, or automation,
is like a recipe card.

133
00:06:50,325 --> 00:06:54,175
Step one, step two, step three,
always in the same order.

134
00:06:54,447 --> 00:06:58,407
This little one says: every
morning, read the stock from Odoo.

135
00:06:58,447 --> 00:07:01,747
Then this box, called
an IF, checks one rule.

136
00:07:01,787 --> 00:07:05,027
Is the quantity on hand
smaller than the reorder point?

137
00:07:05,067 --> 00:07:06,787
If yes, send an email.

138
00:07:06,827 --> 00:07:11,489
If no, do nothing. Useful, and
lots of companies have one.

139
00:07:11,529 --> 00:07:15,609
But it follows the rule exactly,
even when the rule is wrong.

140
00:07:15,649 --> 00:07:19,489
If the pallet jack wheels are at
twenty-six, one above the reorder point

141
00:07:19,529 --> 00:07:23,929
of twenty-five, it stays quiet, even
though the supplier is in Shenzhen,

142
00:07:23,969 --> 00:07:26,689
it's raining and there's a holiday coming.

143
00:07:26,729 --> 00:07:29,529
It can't think "hmm, this one is risky".

144
00:07:29,569 --> 00:07:31,509
It doesn't know what "risky" means.

145
00:07:31,549 --> 00:07:33,095
It only knows the recipe.

146
00:07:33,455 --> 00:07:34,995
And third, the agent.

147
00:07:35,035 --> 00:07:36,525
Let's open Stocky's brain.

148
00:07:36,801 --> 00:07:40,721
The best way to think about an
AI agent is a smart new intern.

149
00:07:40,761 --> 00:07:44,941
A good intern can read, think, use
the tools you give them and take

150
00:07:44,981 --> 00:07:49,281
action. But they need clear
instructions, and they need supervision,

151
00:07:49,321 --> 00:07:50,891
especially in their first few weeks.

152
00:07:51,251 --> 00:07:52,931
Here are Stocky's instructions.

153
00:07:52,971 --> 00:07:58,091
In plain English. "You are Stocky,
BrightLeaf Supplies' inventory and

154
00:07:58,131 --> 00:07:59,631
procurement assistant.

155
00:07:59,671 --> 00:08:02,711
Check every product
against its reorder point.

156
00:08:02,751 --> 00:08:07,991
For suppliers outside the US, consider
weather, currency and holidays.

157
00:08:08,031 --> 00:08:11,631
Never send a purchase order
without Sarah's approval.

158
00:08:11,671 --> 00:08:13,761
Explain your reasoning in plain English."

159
00:08:13,985 --> 00:08:16,825
And underneath, the tools
it's allowed to use.

160
00:08:16,865 --> 00:08:18,625
It can look things up in Odoo.

161
00:08:18,665 --> 00:08:20,165
It can check the weather.

162
00:08:20,205 --> 00:08:22,905
It can check exchange rates and holidays.

163
00:08:22,945 --> 00:08:24,335
It can write to the log.

164
00:08:24,631 --> 00:08:26,271
So the difference is this.

165
00:08:26,311 --> 00:08:28,771
The automation follows a fixed recipe.

166
00:08:28,811 --> 00:08:33,091
The agent gets a goal, a set of
tools and some rules, and it decides

167
00:08:33,131 --> 00:08:36,471
which tools to use and in
what order to reach the goal.

168
00:08:36,511 --> 00:08:39,871
That's why it could spot the
risk on the pallet jack wheels.

169
00:08:39,911 --> 00:08:43,611
Nobody wrote a rule saying "if
Shenzhen plus rain plus holiday,

170
00:08:43,651 --> 00:08:45,593
then warn". It worked that out.

171
00:08:45,913 --> 00:08:47,133
Here's the summary.

172
00:08:47,173 --> 00:08:50,033
A chatbot talks. An automation follows.

173
00:08:50,073 --> 00:08:51,983
An agent reasons and acts.

174
00:08:52,239 --> 00:08:54,199
And one honest note.

175
00:08:54,239 --> 00:08:56,779
Agents are not always the right answer.

176
00:08:56,819 --> 00:09:01,219
If a task is the same every single
time, with a clear rule, a plain

177
00:09:01,259 --> 00:09:04,799
automation is cheaper,
faster and more predictable.

178
00:09:04,839 --> 00:09:08,879
Use an agent when the task needs
a bit of judgement: reading messy

179
00:09:08,919 --> 00:09:12,819
information, weighing up several
factors, explaining a decision.

180
00:09:12,859 --> 00:09:15,469
We'll practise telling them
apart in your activity.

181
00:09:16,158 --> 00:09:18,048
All right, let's try something.

182
00:09:18,096 --> 00:09:21,491
Now that we have looked at chatbots,
automations, and AI agents,

183
00:09:21,531 --> 00:09:25,126
I want to see if you can identify
where each one actually makes sense.

184
00:09:25,166 --> 00:09:27,346
I'll give three situation from BrightLeaf.

185
00:09:27,481 --> 00:09:30,878
Think about each one and decide
whether you would use a chatbot,

186
00:09:30,918 --> 00:09:33,248
a simple automation, or an AI agent.

187
00:09:33,571 --> 00:09:34,741
Here's the first one.

188
00:09:34,917 --> 00:09:38,797
Every night, BrightLeaf need to copy
the previous day's sales order from

189
00:09:38,837 --> 00:09:40,493
Odoo into a Google Sheet.

190
00:09:40,533 --> 00:09:44,666
Second situation, someone needs to
go through 40 supplier emails and

191
00:09:44,706 --> 00:09:48,066
identify which shipments are delayed
and figure out how those delays

192
00:09:48,106 --> 00:09:50,196
could affect the next week's inventory.

193
00:09:50,236 --> 00:09:54,551
And a third one, Sarah needs help
rewriting an email to a supplier who's

194
00:09:54,591 --> 00:09:55,933
already late with the delivery.

195
00:09:55,973 --> 00:09:58,789
She wants the email to
sound polite but also firm.

196
00:09:58,830 --> 00:10:01,100
Take a moment and think
about your answers.

197
00:10:01,150 --> 00:10:02,674
You can pause this video here.

198
00:10:02,714 --> 00:10:04,144
I'll give you a few seconds.

199
00:10:11,068 --> 00:10:12,478
Let's go through them.

200
00:10:12,548 --> 00:10:16,199
For the first one, copying sales
orders every night, I'd use a simple

201
00:10:16,239 --> 00:10:19,568
automation. It's the same task,
following up the same steps every single

202
00:10:19,608 --> 00:10:22,892
day. There's no real need for an
AI agent to make a decision here.

203
00:10:22,932 --> 00:10:25,942
For the second one, I
would use an AI agent.

204
00:10:26,052 --> 00:10:29,864
Why? Because supplier emails are
not always written in the same way.

205
00:10:29,990 --> 00:10:33,180
Some suppliers might
explicitly say there's a delay.

206
00:10:33,346 --> 00:10:35,893
Other might mention a problem
without directly saying it.

207
00:10:35,933 --> 00:10:39,285
We need something that can understand
those messages, connect the

208
00:10:39,325 --> 00:10:42,652
information with inventory levels,
and work out what could be the impact

209
00:10:42,692 --> 00:10:45,084
be. That's where an agent makes sense.

210
00:10:45,150 --> 00:10:47,590
And the third one, that's a chatbot.

211
00:10:47,779 --> 00:10:49,992
Sarah just needs help rewriting an email.

212
00:10:50,032 --> 00:10:54,314
She can open a Claude or ChatGPT,
explain what she wants, and get a

213
00:10:54,354 --> 00:10:58,118
draft back. No complicated
workflow or ERP integration needed.

214
00:10:58,158 --> 00:11:01,818
So the important thing here is
that you don't need an AI agent for

215
00:11:01,858 --> 00:11:04,659
everything. Sometimes a
simple automation is enough.

216
00:11:04,902 --> 00:11:06,650
Sometimes a chatbot does the job.

217
00:11:06,690 --> 00:11:10,040
The real skill is knowing which
one to use for which problem.

218
00:11:10,120 --> 00:11:13,860
And that's exactly what you will
practice in this activity that comes

219
00:11:13,900 --> 00:11:15,010
with this video.

220
00:11:16,113 --> 00:11:19,853
Every agent you'll ever build,
including Stocky, has the same five

221
00:11:19,893 --> 00:11:22,663
parts. Let's go through
them using our intern.

222
00:11:23,039 --> 00:11:25,079
Part one: the brain.

223
00:11:25,119 --> 00:11:29,239
That's the AI model, things
like Claude or ChatGPT.

224
00:11:29,279 --> 00:11:32,099
You'll hear people call this an "LLM".

225
00:11:32,139 --> 00:11:36,219
It just means the type of AI
that reads and writes language.

226
00:11:36,259 --> 00:11:38,459
Think of it as the intern's brain.

227
00:11:38,499 --> 00:11:41,379
It reads, it reasons, it writes.

228
00:11:41,419 --> 00:11:45,429
On its own, as we saw, it's
stuck in a room with no doors.

229
00:11:45,749 --> 00:11:47,849
Part two: the instructions.

230
00:11:47,889 --> 00:11:50,649
This is the briefing you
give the intern on day one.

231
00:11:50,689 --> 00:11:54,249
Who you are, what your job
is, what you must never do.

232
00:11:54,289 --> 00:11:57,309
The clearer this is, the
better the agent behaves.

233
